## Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues of acme/shop on the GitHub Projects board of acme, project 3, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml` (preset `sprints`). Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of `acme/shop`; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

### Lifecycle

`Backlog` → `In progress` → (`Waiting`) → (`Ready`) → `Done`, through `start`, `wait`, `ready`, `done`. A status in parentheses may be skipped. Each verb below says which statuses it moves from and to.

- `Backlog` is the initial status: `gh dojo new` puts new issues there, and an issue without a status or not on the board counts as `Backlog`.
- Final: `Done`. `gh dojo list` hides issues there unless `--all`.
- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, when it is closed or its board status is `Ready` or `Done`: its work is done though the issue may be open.
- A blocker whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) counts as unresolved.
- A sub-issue is resolved, or not, by the same rule.

### Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N`; `--dry-run` prints its plan (status, links, the comment it would post) without changing anything, and `--show` also prints the issue's card, as `gh dojo show N` does. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line, such as `#42 Backlog → In progress, comment left`: there is no need to read the issue again.

`N…` takes several numbers at once: one that fails is skipped and named in the summary.

#### `start`

**When:** You begin working on the issue

`gh dojo start N…`: Backlog, Waiting, Ready → In progress; optional --comment; sprint: today's iteration; comments "Started. Session: {session} {comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.

#### `wait`

**When:** Work is stuck: waiting for another issue or an outside decision

`gh dojo wait N…`: In progress → Waiting; needs --by or --comment; comments "Waiting for {by}. {comment}".

- `--by 12` also marks the issue blocked by #12; text (`--by "the user's decision"`) only goes into the comment.

#### `ready`

**When:** The work is done, verified and committed, but not released yet

`gh dojo ready N…`: In progress, Waiting → Ready; sprint: the iteration of the day it entered Ready; comments only with --comment.

- With an unresolved sub-issue it warns on stderr and still runs: read `gh dojo show N` and finish the sub-issues first, or ask the user.
- In one run with its sub-issues (`gh dojo ready 40 41 42`) the sub-issues it moves count as resolved: they go first, and the parent is refused only if one of them is.

#### `done`

**When:** The change is released

`gh dojo done N…`: Ready, In progress → Done; also on closed issues; sprint: the iteration of the day it entered Ready; comments only with --comment; closes as completed.

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a sub-issue is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Finish them first, or ask the user; never move the status another way.
- In one run with its sub-issues (`gh dojo done 40 41 42`) the sub-issues it moves count as resolved: they go first, and the parent is refused only if one of them is.
- For a release, `gh dojo release <tag> --verb done --dry-run` lists the issues the release includes and its plan; read `gh dojo release --help` before you run it.

#### `drop`

**When:** The issue is not needed or a duplicate

`gh dojo drop N…`: any status → status unchanged; needs --comment; on a closed issue only if closed as not planned; comments "{comment}"; closes as not planned.

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.

#### `approve`

**When:** The user approved the change

`gh dojo approve N…`: any status → status unchanged; labels +approved.

#### `park`

**When:** Work on the issue stops for now

`gh dojo park N…`: any status → status unchanged; labels +parked.

#### `unpark`

**When:** Work on the issue goes on

`gh dojo unpark N…`: any status → status unchanged; labels -parked.

#### `ship`

**When:** The approved change goes out

`gh dojo ship N…`: any status → Done; also on closed issues; refused without the label approved; refused with the label parked.

- It is refused (exit code 2) unless the issue has the label `approved`: `gh dojo show N` lists its labels. `approve` adds `approved`. Never add a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `parked`. `unpark` removes `parked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- For a release, `gh dojo release <tag> --verb ship --dry-run` lists the issues the release includes and its plan; read `gh dojo release --help` before you run it.

### Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues in `Backlog` or without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--sprint current`. Once you pick one, `gh dojo start N --show`: it takes the issue and prints its card.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue (to take it as well, `gh dojo start N --show` prints the same card): its status, sprint, milestone, labels, blockers both ways (unresolved first, each with its board status), its parent and sub-issues (the same way), status history, description and the last 5 comments; a line left out means none. Several numbers, `gh dojo show 12 13`, print three lines per issue: read the one you take alone. `--comments all` prints the whole thread (up to 100), `--full` everything uncut; a verb takes both too and prints the card with them, as with `--show`.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted; `Ready` is one line of numbers, `--status Ready` lists them with titles. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--sprint current`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.
- `gh dojo next` and `gh dojo list` read the board a few seconds behind writes: an issue just created or moved may be missing or in its old status. To check one issue, `gh dojo show N`.

### Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <type> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board in `Backlog`, in one command; a long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. More labels, assignees, a milestone, blockers or a parent: `gh dojo new --help`. `--type bug` adds the label `type: bug`; the types are the repository's labels starting with `type: `, and an unknown one fails listing them.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` / `gh dojo unblock N --by M`: add or remove a blocked-by link without changing the status. An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M`.
- A commit pushed without a pull request names its issue after a keyword in its message, as `Fixes #12`; parentheses at the end of the first line, `(#12)`, do not count here: that is how `gh dojo release` finds the issues a release includes.

### Repeats are safe

Run the same command again after an error: a verb does not set a status that is already set, nor post a comment that is already the latest from gh-dojo; `comment` does not post its last comment again; `block` and `unblock` skip links that are already as asked; `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created instead of creating another.

### Exit codes

On an error, read its `error:`, `allowed:` and `fix:` lines and do what `fix:` says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later.

### JSON

Every command takes `--json`, `--jq` and `--template`, as gh does, for picking values out; for everyday work the text is shorter.

## Our rules

Run `make test` before `ready`.
